{"ts": 1789777500.4484968, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've now covered all 40 features in the third slot. Feature 29 wins decisively at 1.048; the runner-up field clusters tightly at 0.76\u20130.96, exactly the pattern expected when one addition carries real signal and the rest add only noise.\n\nNow for the OOS prediction. Key considerations for the shrinkage:\n\n**Selection bias.** I ran ~80 evaluations and picked the max. But the winner is not a lucky draw from a flat field \u2014 its three components each have standalone Sharpes of 0.50\u20130.67, far outside the \u00b10.34 noise band of the other 37 features. The combination's edge is driven by identifiable individual signal plus genuine decorrelation, so the selection-induced inflation is modest rather than the near-total shrinkage warranted when a winner is plucked from undifferentiated noise.\n\n**Sampling error.** With n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22, so in-sample estimation noise alone is material.\n\n**Residual optimization.** I did tune signs and the third slot, worth a few points of shrinkage. Netting these: I expect meaningful but not severe decay, centering around 0.85.\n\nFor the SD, I need to account for more than sampling error: the OOS period may have a different regime, and feature relationships can decay. An SD of 0.30 gives a distribution that comfortably spans a disappointing ~0.55 through a maintained ~1.15 \u2014 honest about the possibility that some of the 1.048 is in-sample luck, without pretending to precision I don't have."}
