{"ts": 1789777543.758668, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Specification:** long feature 19, long feature 25, short feature 29 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.048**.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.85 \u00b1 0.30**\n\n### Method\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Two stood out clearly: f19 (0.670) and f25 (0.641). The remaining 38 fell within \u00b10.34, consistent with noise.\n\n2. **Tested pairs.** f19+f25 reached 0.926, a real gain over either alone. This also surfaced something the univariate screen would have missed: **feature 29 was the most valuable partner for both** (0.824 with f19, 0.810 with f25) despite ranking as the *worst* single feature at \u22120.495. Its sign was simply inverted \u2014 short f29 is a 0.495 standalone signal in its own right.\n\n3. **Exhaustively tested all 40 features in the third slot.** f29 won decisively at 1.048, with the entire runner-up field clustered at 0.76\u20130.96.\n\n4. **Verified sign choices.** Flipping any single sign collapses performance (1.048 \u2192 0.27\u20130.47), confirming the configuration sits at a sharp, well-identified optimum rather than on a flat plateau.\n\n### Why I shrank 1.048 \u2192 0.85\n\nThe honest question is how much of the in-sample figure survives contact with new data. Three factors pull it down:\n\n- **Selection bias** \u2014 I took the max over ~80 evaluations. This is the main concern, but it's mitigated here: the winner isn't a lucky draw from a flat field. Each of its three components has a standalone Sharpe of 0.50\u20130.67, far outside the noise band of the other 37 features. The edge traces to identifiable individual signal plus genuine decorrelation, so the inflation is moderate rather than the near-total shrinkage appropriate when a winner emerges from undifferentiated noise.\n- **Sampling error** \u2014 at n=5000, the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22 even with no selection at all.\n- **Residual tuning** \u2014 sign and third-slot optimization costs a few more points.\n\nThe \u00b10.30 SD spans a disappointing ~0.55 to a maintained ~1.15. I deliberately set it wider than pure sampling error, since regime shift and signal decay are real risks that in-sample statistics cannot measure.\n\n**One caveat worth flagging:** every number above comes from a single in-sample period with no held-out validation available through this interface. The f29 sign-flip finding is a good reminder that univariate screening alone would have discarded the most useful feature in the set \u2014 combination effects mattered more than individual rankings here."}
